## Early intervention in psychosis

If you have a first episode of psychosis, you should be offered care from an **early intervention in psychosis (EIP)** team. They offer medicine, talking therapy, family support and help with work, education and housing.

## Antipsychotic medicines

**Antipsychotics** reduce symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions. They come as tablets, liquids or **long-acting injections** given every few weeks.

- your doctor will talk through the options and side effects with you
- common side effects include **weight gain**, drowsiness and restlessness
- it can take a few weeks to feel the full benefit
- **do not stop suddenly**, as this can lead to a relapse. Talk to your team if you want to change or stop

If two antipsychotics have not worked well enough, you should be offered **clozapine**.

## Talking therapies

- **CBT for psychosis (CBTp)** helps you make sense of and cope with your experiences
- **family intervention** helps families understand psychosis and support recovery
- **arts therapies** can help, especially with negative symptoms

## Physical health

People with schizophrenia have a higher risk of heart disease and diabetes, partly because of some medicines. You should have **regular physical health checks**, including weight, blood pressure, blood sugar and cholesterol, and support to stop smoking.

## Hospital

Some people need a stay in hospital during a crisis, usually voluntarily. In some situations, the **Mental Health Act** allows people to be treated in hospital to keep them or others safe.

